  "notes": "West leads the ♦6 against 3NT. South has only 5 top tricks, the ♠A, 2 hearts, the ♦A, and the ♣A. There are finessing positions in spades, diamonds, and clubs which could provide extra tricks. South's long clubs are the most promising trick source.\n\nIf dummy's ♦Q is finessed at trick 1, the contract is in danger. East may win the ♦K and return a spade. South might as well finesse but West could win the ♠K and clear the suit. South will still need to establish his clubs. If West has the ♣K, making that finesse lose, the defense may be in a position to cash enough spade tricks to defeat the contract.\n\nSouth was unlucky to lose 3 of 3 finesses, but South can guarantee the contract by taking the ♦A at trick 1. The ♣J is led for a finesse. If the ♣J holds, South continues with the ♣10.\n\nIn this way, South sets up at least 4 club tricks without losing the lead to East (who can switch to an unfriendly spade). If West has the ♣K he cannot profitably attack spades from his side of the table.\n\nIf West wins the ♣K and exits a diamond to the ♦Q and East's ♦K, East can play a spade. It is too late, however, as the ♦J is high and represents South's ninth trick.\n\nIf West exits in hearts instead of diamonds, South wins and plays diamonds himself. By playing this way, the contract is certain even if all 3 finesses lose.",
  "commentary_paragraphs": [
    "West leads the ♦6 against 3NT. South has only 5 top tricks, the ♠A, 2 hearts, the ♦A, and the ♣A. There are finessing positions in spades, diamonds, and clubs which could provide extra tricks. South's long clubs are the most promising trick source.",
    "If dummy's ♦Q is finessed at trick 1, the contract is in danger. East may win the ♦K and return a spade. South might as well finesse but West could win the ♠K and clear the suit. South will still need to establish his clubs. If West has the ♣K, making that finesse lose, the defense may be in a position to cash enough spade tricks to defeat the contract.",
    "South was unlucky to lose 3 of 3 finesses, but South can guarantee the contract by taking the ♦A at trick 1. The ♣J is led for a finesse. If the ♣J holds, South continues with the ♣10.",
    "In this way, South sets up at least 4 club tricks without losing the lead to East (who can switch to an unfriendly spade). If West has the ♣K he cannot profitably attack spades from his side of the table.",
    "If West wins the ♣K and exits a diamond to the ♦Q and East's ♦K, East can play a spade. It is too late, however, as the ♦J is high and represents South's ninth trick.",
    "If West exits in hearts instead of diamonds, South wins and plays diamonds himself. By playing this way, the contract is certain even if all 3 finesses lose."
